# Editor backups
*.swp
*~
\#*\#
.\#*

# `quality-gate --fix` writes `<file>.<timestamp>.backup` beside anything it rewrites.
# Git already holds the previous version, so these are transient by construction — but they
# are also untracked, so without this every fix run leaves the tree dirty and invites one
# into a commit.
*.backup

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/

# Quality gate output
latestReport.json

# Stale local corpus (telemetry now goes to org-judgement-corpus)
.ijs-corpus/
build/

# Private companions, cloned as siblings and never committed here.
#
# `project/` holds the master plan, proposals and session summaries;
# `development-guidelines/` is the shared framework. Both are private, and these two
# entries are what keeps that true. The rule exists because it was learned the
# expensive way: a disclosure scan before publishing found a client's name in 44 files
# and 45 commits, roughly 25 of them under `project/`. Nothing there was careless — a
# proposal names the client because that is why the checker exists. The boundary is
# structural so the judgement does not have to be made sentence by sentence.
#
# Cloning either one *inside* this tree is the failure mode these lines catch. If a
# .git appears under development-guidelines/, something re-cloned it in place and this
# repository has a nested repository again.
/project/
/development-guidelines/
/.claude/skills/

# Python bytecode from scripts/benchmarks/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
